# Add an NVMe Key Value backend to NIXL: the vendor-neutral half of DOCA MEMOS.
#
# NIXL's DOCA_MEMOS plugin (ai-dynamo/nixl#1717, still open) is a KV-cache
# backend whose store the host sees as an NVMe KV namespace. It reaches that
# namespace through NVIDIA's doca-kv library and needs DOCA 4.4 and a
# BlueField-3, so on a ROCm image it cannot be built, let alone run.
#
# But its own README gives the game away: `device_name` is `/dev/nvme0n1` -- a
# KERNEL device. The NVM Express Key Value Command Set is ratified, Linux
# attaches a KV namespace, and the commands are the same commands whoever
# issues them. So this plugin keeps DOCA_MEMOS's NIXL-facing semantics exactly
# and replaces the vendor library with io_uring NVMe passthrough.
#
# WHAT LINUX ACTUALLY DOES WITH A KV NAMESPACE
#
# This was the one assumption worth checking before writing anything, because
# everything depends on it. In nvme_update_ns_info() an unrecognised Command
# Set Identifier takes the default arm:
#
#   dev_info(... "block device for nsid %u not supported (csi %u)\n" ...);
#   ret = nvme_update_ns_info_generic(ns, info);     /* returns -ENODEV */
#   ...
#   /* If probing fails due an unsupported feature, hide the block device,
#    * but still allow other access. */
#   if (ret == -ENODEV) { ns->disk->flags |= GENHD_FL_HIDDEN; ... ret = 0; }
#
# The namespace is attached, only the BLOCK device is hidden, and /dev/ngXnY
# is there and takes passthrough. Confirmed on the cluster against SPDK's KV
# target: `nvme nvme2: block device for nsid 1 not supported (csi 1)`, and
# /dev/ng2n1 appears. No DOCA anywhere.
#
# THE NIXL MAPPING, WHICH IS DOCA_MEMOS'S
#
# One descriptor pair is one value: the local DRAM_SEG buffer is the value and
# the remote OBJ_SEG descriptor names the key. NIXL_WRITE is Store, NIXL_READ
# is Retrieve, queryMem() is one Exist per descriptor. The key is the devId
# bytes when metaInfo is empty, otherwise metaInfo as hex of up to 16 bytes,
# or any string hashed to 128 bits with convert_key_to_128bit. A Retrieve
# whose stored length differs from the buffer fails. All of that is
# DOCA_MEMOS's contract, so an application written against one can move to the
# other.
#
# One difference, stated because it is the kind that bites silently:
# DOCA_MEMOS hashes with unkeyed Blake2b and this uses FNV-1a over two halves,
# to avoid pulling libb2 in for a single call. A local KV namespace is not a
# security boundary and a collision costs an overwrite rather than a
# vulnerability -- but two clients sharing one namespace MUST agree, so
# interoperating with a DOCA_MEMOS client means making this Blake2b. That is
# the one thing the two could disagree on and still both appear to work.
#
# THE OPCODES OVERLAP THE BLOCK COMMAND SET, AND THAT IS THE DANGER
#
# KV Store is 01h and Retrieve is 02h -- the same values as Write and Read in
# the NVM command set. Pointed at an LBA namespace by a typo in `device_name`,
# a Store does not fail: it executes as a block write and destroys the start of
# the device. So openDevice() reads the Command Set Identifier out of the
# namespace's Identify data (CNS 03h, descriptor NIDT 04h) and refuses anything
# that is not KV, before a command can be built. That check is the single most
# important line in the plugin.
#
# WHY io_uring AND NOT ioctl
#
# NVME_IOCTL_IO_CMD is synchronous: every value would block a thread for a
# fabric round trip, and NIXL's prepare/post/check interface exists precisely
# so that it need not. IORING_OP_URING_CMD carrying NVME_URING_CMD_IO is the
# same command submitted asynchronously, so postXfer() returns NIXL_IN_PROG
# with everything in flight. The ring needs SQE128 and CQE32, and neither is
# tuning: a passthrough command does not fit a 64-byte SQE, and the NVMe
# completion result -- which is how a Retrieve reports the stored value length
# -- does not fit a 16-byte CQE.
#
# VRAM_SEG IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. NVMe passthrough hands the kernel a user
# virtual address to pin, and a device pointer is not that; it would need
# p2pdma, which neither the emulated device here nor a stock KV namespace
# offers. DOCA_MEMOS makes the same choice, and additionally requires
# hugepages for the same underlying reason.
#
# NIXLBENCH
#
# --backend=NVME_KV with --nvme_kv_device=/dev/ngXnY. A READ run has to seed
# its value first, and nixlbench seeds through an external CLI rather than
# through NIXL -- `aws` for S3, `nvme` here. There is no nvme-cli subcommand
# for the KV command set, so the seeding builds the raw passthrough itself,
# with the key split across CDW2/3 and CDW14/15 exactly as the plugin does it.
# For this backend the object name IS the key, written as 32 hex characters,
# so no hash has to agree between the benchmark and the plugin -- and
# --nvme_kv_convert_key refuses to seed rather than seeding the wrong key.
#
# ONE BUG WORTH RECORDING, BECAUSE IT LOOKED LIKE A RESULT
#
# The first working version tagged each SQE with the task's index within its
# request. One ring serves every request, so every request numbered its tasks
# from zero and a straggling completion from an earlier transfer was
# indistinguishable from this one's first task: checkXfer() counted it and
# declared the transfer finished before the device had answered.
#
# It did not look like a bug. It looked like 23 GB/s at 128 KiB with a 2.8 us
# latency, over an emulated NIC -- which is what gave it away, together with
# an "Avg Tx 0.0 us" in the same table. The fix is a token unique for the
# engine's lifetime and a map from it back to the request and task that
# submitted it, so a completion can always find its owner. releaseReqH()
# moves the tokens of a request abandoned in flight to an orphan set, because
# those completions still arrive and following a freed handle would be worse
# than the original bug.
#
# VRAM_SEG, AND WHY IT IS STAGED RATHER THAN DIRECT
#
# NVMe passthrough maps its data buffer with blk_rq_map_user_io() or
# io_uring_cmd_import_fixed() (drivers/nvme/host/ioctl.c), and both pin USER
# PAGES. A device pointer is not backed by any, so there is nothing to pin,
# and there is no dmabuf or p2pdma path in the passthrough ioctl to take one
# instead. IORING_URING_CMD_FIXED exists, but a registered io_uring buffer is
# an iovec of user addresses too.
#
# Nor would p2pdma help on the transports used here even if it existed. Over
# nvme-rdma and nvme-tcp the HOST moves the data -- nvme_rdma_map_data()
# builds an sg_table from the request's bio pages and contains no dmabuf path
# at all, nvme-tcp sends from those same pages -- so the bytes have to be in
# host memory at some point regardless.
#
# THE LIMIT IS THE KERNEL CLIENT, NOT THE FABRIC, and the distinction is
# worth keeping straight. rocm-ernic's NVMe-oF target reaches guest memory
# with dp_nvmeof_from_host(), which is dp_sge_to_host() -- the same rkey path
# its S3 backend uses, and the one that already RDMAs straight into GPU
# memory for the hipObject engine. The emulated hardware can therefore do
# GPU-direct KV today. What cannot get there is any client that goes through
# the kernel's NVMe stack, because that stack can only describe host pages.
# Closing it needs a userspace initiator that registers VRAM with
# ibv_reg_dmabuf_mr and puts that rkey in the command's keyed SGL itself.
#
# So a VRAM registration gets a pinned host staging buffer (hipHostMalloc, so
# the pages are already pinned when the kernel maps them again for the NVMe
# command), a Store copies device-to-host before submitting, and a Retrieve
# copies host-to-device once the device has answered. Both copies run OUTSIDE
# the ring lock: hipMemcpy blocks, and holding the lock across it would
# serialise every other request behind one GPU copy. The host-to-device copy
# happens in checkXfer() before it reports success, which is what keeps
# NIXL_SUCCESS meaning "the data is where the caller asked for it".
#
# This is what hipObject does when a device pointer cannot be registered with
# the NIC (hipObjBufSync, HIPOBJ_SYNC_TO_HOST / _TO_DEVICE). DOCA_MEMOS avoids
# the question by taking DRAM only -- and requires hugepages even for that,
# because the NVMe kernel device hands buffers over by user address and a
# value spread across 4 KiB pages can exceed the per-command segment limit.
#
# The copy is real and it is in the numbers; cluster/README.md has them beside
# the DRAM ones rather than reporting VRAM as if it were free. What the
# caller gets is that a device pointer works at all, without arranging the
# staging itself.
